1
00:00:00,102 --> 00:00:01,647
Nineteen seventy-three.

2
00:00:02,448 --> 00:00:07,717
In Rome, a sixteen-year-old boy is dragged
into a car by masked men and vanishes.

3
00:00:08,516 --> 00:00:11,051
A few days later, a letter arrives.

4
00:00:11,849 --> 00:00:14,670
The kidnappers want seventeen million
dollars.

5
00:00:15,469 --> 00:00:20,725
His grandfather is the richest private
citizen in America, and he gives a famous
answer.

6
00:00:21,523 --> 00:00:28,446
He says he has fourteen other
grandchildren, and if he pays one ransom,
he will have fourteen more kidnapped
grandchildren.

7
00:00:29,245 --> 00:00:32,847
Months later, a package arrives at a
newspaper office.

8
00:00:33,646 --> 00:00:35,399
Inside is a human ear.

9
00:00:36,198 --> 00:00:38,173
His name is Jean Paul Getty.

10
00:00:38,971 --> 00:00:44,345
And the question that haunts his story is
simple: why did the richest man in the
world refuse to pay?

11
00:00:45,270 --> 00:00:51,529
Jean Paul Getty was born on December
fifteenth, eighteen ninety-two, in
Minneapolis, Minnesota.

12
00:00:52,329 --> 00:00:57,351
His father, George, was a lawyer who had
moved the family into the booming oil
business,

13
00:00:57,499 --> 00:01:00,086
and strict Methodist discipline ruled the
house.

14
00:01:00,884 --> 00:01:04,708
Young Paul was bright, restless, and
spoiled by his mother.

15
00:01:05,507 --> 00:01:12,872
He was sent to boarding school in
California, studied at Oxford, and spent
his early twenties more interested in
parties than in work.

16
00:01:13,671 --> 00:01:15,255
But his father had a rule.

17
00:01:16,053 --> 00:01:18,758
If Paul wanted money, he would have to
earn it.

18
00:01:19,556 --> 00:01:25,515
And in nineteen fifteen, Paul went to
Oklahoma, where oil fever was sweeping
across the plains,

19
00:01:25,728 --> 00:01:29,695
and he began buying leases, the right to
drill on a piece of land.

20
00:01:30,494 --> 00:01:36,518
He lived in dirty boarding houses, wore
muddy boots, and negotiated with farmers
face to face.

21
00:01:37,316 --> 00:01:43,927
In nineteen sixteen, a well he had a stake
in came in, and in a single year he made
his first million dollars.

22
00:01:44,725 --> 00:01:46,036
He was twenty-four.

23
00:01:46,950 --> 00:01:51,451
At twenty-four, with a million dollars,
Getty announced that he was retiring.

24
00:01:52,252 --> 00:01:56,245
He moved to Los Angeles, and for two years
he lived like a playboy,

25
00:01:56,444 --> 00:01:58,576
chasing women and spending his money.

26
00:01:59,374 --> 00:02:01,479
It was a mistake, and he knew it.

27
00:02:02,278 --> 00:02:05,880
He went back to work, and this time he
learned the real rules.

28
00:02:06,679 --> 00:02:08,511
He studied balance sheets.

29
00:02:09,309 --> 00:02:10,958
He read every contract.

30
00:02:11,757 --> 00:02:16,505
He learned that in the oil business, the
winners were the men who bought when
others panicked.

31
00:02:17,304 --> 00:02:21,948
In the nineteen thirties, during the Great
Depression, oil was almost worthless.

32
00:02:22,746 --> 00:02:24,083
Companies were failing.

33
00:02:24,882 --> 00:02:28,784
Getty began buying their shares, quietly,
for pennies.

34
00:02:29,582 --> 00:02:36,570
He bought the Pacific Western Oil company,
and later Tidewater and Skelly, building a
company slowly from the ruins.

35
00:02:37,369 --> 00:02:41,961
His father had left him a smaller fortune
than expected, but Getty needed no one.

36
00:02:42,759 --> 00:02:47,560
By the end of the thirties, he was a major
oil man, and he still wanted more.

37
00:02:48,358 --> 00:02:52,182
In nineteen thirty-two, he found a bargain
nobody else wanted.

38
00:02:52,981 --> 00:02:58,654
A giant company called Pacific Western Oil
owned huge fields, and its shares had
collapsed.

39
00:02:59,452 --> 00:03:03,719
Getty bought them, bit by bit, and then he
forced his way into the board.

40
00:03:04,517 --> 00:03:07,300
By the end of the decade he had a
controlling stake.

41
00:03:08,098 --> 00:03:14,331
He understood something that other people
missed: in a crisis, the rich do not make
money by working harder.

42
00:03:15,129 --> 00:03:18,341
They make money by buying what the
frightened are throwing away.

43
00:03:19,254 --> 00:03:21,216
The next move changed everything.

44
00:03:22,017 --> 00:03:26,361
In nineteen forty-nine, Getty took a risk
that frightened his own advisers.

45
00:03:27,160 --> 00:03:33,288
He went to the Middle East, to the Neutral
Zone, a barren strip of desert between
Saudi Arabia and Kuwait,

46
00:03:33,475 --> 00:03:35,671
where no one believed there was any oil.

47
00:03:36,470 --> 00:03:38,328
The Saudi king wanted money.

48
00:03:39,126 --> 00:03:43,419
Getty offered a deal, a payment of nine
and a half million dollars up front,

49
00:03:43,618 --> 00:03:47,742
and a million dollars a year in rent,
whether or not any oil was found.

50
00:03:48,540 --> 00:03:49,851
It was a huge bet.

51
00:03:50,649 --> 00:03:54,044
For four years, his company drilled and
found nothing.

52
00:03:54,842 --> 00:03:56,882
His directors begged him to stop.

53
00:03:57,681 --> 00:03:58,914
Getty refused.

54
00:03:59,712 --> 00:04:03,275
He had already seen that the geology
matched fields nearby.

55
00:04:04,087 --> 00:04:07,351
In nineteen fifty-three, the well finally
came in.

56
00:04:08,149 --> 00:04:12,807
It produced oil in vast quantities, and
Getty owned the rights almost alone.

57
00:04:13,605 --> 00:04:16,661
The bet that looked like madness had made
him a billionaire.

58
00:04:17,459 --> 00:04:20,385
The partnership with Saudi Arabia was not
easy.

59
00:04:21,183 --> 00:04:27,182
His rivals, the giant American companies,
had spent decades building relationships
in the Middle East,

60
00:04:27,368 --> 00:04:30,919
and they laughed at the small independent
who wanted to drill in the desert.

61
00:04:31,717 --> 00:04:38,262
Getty's engineers crossed the sand in
trucks, sleeping in tents, and drilled
well after well that came up dry.

62
00:04:39,061 --> 00:04:41,270
Each dry hole cost him a fortune.

63
00:04:42,069 --> 00:04:45,164
Each month, he still paid his million-
dollar rent.

64
00:04:45,962 --> 00:04:48,653
Friends said he was throwing money into
the sand.

65
00:04:49,452 --> 00:04:53,275
Getty only said that the oil was there,
and that patience would pay.

66
00:04:54,198 --> 00:05:01,694
In nineteen fifty-seven, Fortune magazine
named Getty the richest living American,
with a fortune of about one billion
dollars.

67
00:05:02,494 --> 00:05:04,391
He barely changed his life.

68
00:05:05,190 --> 00:05:10,094
Getty moved to England and bought a Tudor
mansion called Sutton Place, outside
London.

69
00:05:10,893 --> 00:05:14,365
He filled it with art, antiques, and rare
carpets.

70
00:05:15,164 --> 00:05:19,951
Then he installed something strange in the
front hall: a pay telephone for guests.

71
00:05:20,750 --> 00:05:23,337
Visitors had to use coins to make calls.

72
00:05:24,135 --> 00:05:28,376
The richest man in the world was unwilling
to let people use his phone for free.

73
00:05:29,174 --> 00:05:33,141
Reporters loved the story, and it followed
him for the rest of his life.

74
00:05:33,940 --> 00:05:39,678
He also became a collector, with a passion
for ancient Greek and Roman statues and
French furniture.

75
00:05:40,476 --> 00:05:47,399
He bought a ranch house in Malibu and
filled it with art, then built a replica
of an ancient Roman villa to hold his
collection.

76
00:05:48,198 --> 00:05:53,923
The museum opened in the nineteen
seventies, and it was the only place where
Getty seemed truly generous.

77
00:05:54,721 --> 00:05:58,753
He was married five times, and every
marriage ended in divorce.

78
00:05:59,552 --> 00:06:03,949
He admitted, in his memoir, that he was a
better businessman than husband.

79
00:06:04,747 --> 00:06:09,717
He said that a man can be successful in
business, or in marriage, but rarely both.

80
00:06:10,638 --> 00:06:12,600
Getty lived by strict rules.

81
00:06:13,401 --> 00:06:18,123
He believed in thrift, hard work, and
above all in controlling costs.

82
00:06:18,921 --> 00:06:23,527
He read financial reports on the toilet
and did his own laundry in hotel sinks.

83
00:06:24,325 --> 00:06:27,381
He paid for his suits to be mended instead
of replaced.

84
00:06:28,179 --> 00:06:31,300
He also gave advice in a book called How
to Be Rich.

85
00:06:32,099 --> 00:06:33,722
His formula was simple.

86
00:06:34,520 --> 00:06:35,584
Rise early.

87
00:06:36,382 --> 00:06:37,251
Work hard.

88
00:06:38,049 --> 00:06:39,152
Strike oil.

89
00:06:39,950 --> 00:06:42,472
And above all, hold on to your cash.

90
00:06:43,270 --> 00:06:46,001
But his behavior with his own family was
cold.

91
00:06:46,799 --> 00:06:50,584
His son Paul Junior struggled with
addiction and a failing marriage.

92
00:06:51,382 --> 00:06:52,628
Getty cut him off.

93
00:06:53,427 --> 00:06:58,501
His grandchildren barely knew him, and he
saw them as obligations, not as family.

94
00:06:59,299 --> 00:07:01,300
He also had strange habits.

95
00:07:02,099 --> 00:07:08,826
He insisted on rooms kept at an exact
temperature, he refused to use an elevator
in a hotel if the stairs were free,

96
00:07:09,026 --> 00:07:11,131
and he kept a guard dog in his bedroom.

97
00:07:11,929 --> 00:07:17,876
He believed that most people wanted to
take advantage of his money, and he
treated friends like possible thieves.

98
00:07:18,674 --> 00:07:21,587
In his own mind, he was protecting his
fortune.

99
00:07:22,385 --> 00:07:27,029
In everyone else's mind, he was protecting
it from the people who should have
mattered most.

100
00:07:27,942 --> 00:07:34,631
Then, in July nineteen seventy-three, the
kidnappers took his sixteen-year-old
grandson, Paul the Third,

101
00:07:34,765 --> 00:07:36,115
off a street in Rome.

102
00:07:36,916 --> 00:07:39,984
The kidnappers demanded seventeen million
dollars.

103
00:07:40,783 --> 00:07:42,016
Getty refused.

104
00:07:42,814 --> 00:07:46,130
He said he would not pay a penny, and the
world was shocked.

105
00:07:46,929 --> 00:07:48,044
Months passed.

106
00:07:48,843 --> 00:07:53,891
In November, an envelope arrived at a
newspaper with a lock of hair and a human
ear.

107
00:07:54,689 --> 00:07:56,508
Only then did Getty relent.

108
00:07:57,306 --> 00:08:04,424
He negotiated the ransom down to about two
point two million dollars, the maximum
amount that he could deduct from his
taxes.

109
00:08:05,223 --> 00:08:08,474
He lent his son the rest, at four percent
interest.

110
00:08:09,272 --> 00:08:11,052
Paul the Third was released.

111
00:08:11,850 --> 00:08:15,961
He lived, but he was never the same, and
the family never recovered.

112
00:08:16,759 --> 00:08:18,240
Getty never visited him.

113
00:08:19,038 --> 00:08:21,612
The scandal changed how the world saw
Getty.

114
00:08:22,410 --> 00:08:25,870
Newspapers called him the miser who let a
boy lose his ear.

115
00:08:26,668 --> 00:08:31,469
Defenders said that if he had paid, every
child in his family would have become a
target.

116
00:08:32,267 --> 00:08:34,906
Historians still argue about who was
right.

117
00:08:35,705 --> 00:08:43,253
Three years later, on June sixth, nineteen
seventy-six, Jean Paul Getty died at
Sutton Place at the age of eighty-three,

118
00:08:43,452 --> 00:08:45,922
with a fortune of about two billion
dollars.

119
00:08:46,830 --> 00:08:50,452
Getty left most of his wealth to a museum
he had built in Malibu,

120
00:08:50,352 --> 00:08:51,266
California.

121
00:08:52,067 --> 00:08:56,958
It became one of the richest art
institutions in the world, and it carries
his name today.

122
00:08:57,757 --> 00:08:59,693
His story has two lessons.

123
00:09:00,491 --> 00:09:02,909
The first is that patience beats panic.

124
00:09:03,707 --> 00:09:06,815
Getty made his fortune by betting while
others ran away.

125
00:09:07,613 --> 00:09:10,318
The second is the cost of counting every
coin.

126
00:09:11,116 --> 00:09:14,966
A man who treats money as the only thing
that matters can win the world,

127
00:09:15,113 --> 00:09:16,958
and still lose everything else.

128
00:09:17,757 --> 00:09:21,841
Money is a tool, and Getty knew how to use
it better than almost anyone.

129
00:09:22,640 --> 00:09:27,622
But he never learned that the people
closest to him were the one thing his
fortune could not replace.

130
00:09:28,421 --> 00:09:34,237
If you enjoyed this story, subscribe,
because tomorrow at six, another file
opens.

